valgrind's arm64 decoder (VEX) marks every plain branch, B, as a call,
like BL. Callgrind then pushes a call-stack frame for each loop jump and
tail call that never returns, and its call stack grows without end: the
long counts on a Raspberry Pi ran out of memory. 32-bit ARM does it
right. Tested on valgrind 3.24.0 and 3.28.0 on a Raspberry Pi 5: the
programs' own instruction counts unchanged (xz and zstd), call stack
normal again.

--- a/VEX/priv/guest_arm64_toIR.c
+++ b/VEX/priv/guest_arm64_toIR.c
@@ -7432,7 +7432,10 @@
       }
       putPC(mkU64(guest_PC_curr_instr + simm64));
       dres->whatNext = Dis_StopHere;
-      dres->jk_StopHere = Ijk_Call;
+      /* Only BL is a call: a plain B is a jump (as on 32-bit ARM). As
+         Ijk_Call, callgrind took every B (loops, tail calls) for a call
+         and its call stack grew without end. */
+      dres->jk_StopHere = bLink ? Ijk_Call : Ijk_Boring;
       DIP("b%s 0x%llx\n", bLink == 1 ? "l" : "",
                           guest_PC_curr_instr + simm64);
       return True;
